  },
  {
   "uuid": "ec38c37d-6ba3-44b3-b5ed-95846b29d35f",
   "text": "",
   "content": [
    {
     "start_timestamp": "2026-03-16T00:30:02.000000Z",
     "stop_timestamp": "2026-03-16T00:30:15.757000Z",
     "type": "text",
     "text": "General picture (check with a tax tool or professional for your case):\n\n- **W-2 (GRA)**: wages, taxed like any job. Goes in normally.\n- **1098-T**: reports tuition billed/paid (box 1) and scholarships/grants (box 5), which usually includes tuition waivers.\n  - If box 5 ≥ box 1, you generally **can't claim an education credit**, because you didn't pay qualified expenses out of pocket.\n  - If box 5 > box 1 (scholarship exceeded tuition and fees), the excess can be **taxable income**.\n  - Most tax software asks you to enter it anyway and works this out.\n- **Tuition waiver for a GRA**: in many cases a tuition reduction for graduate assistants who do teaching or research is excluded from income, but whether it shows in box 5 depends on the school.\n\nSo: enter it, don't skip it, and see what the software concludes. If it says you owe tax on a scholarship you never received as cash, look at whether the waiver was coded as a scholarship.",
     "citations": []
    }
   ],
   "sender": "assistant",
   "index": 1,
